A||N||22||WhiteFieroBlur||Does lowering give you all kinds of trouble?||||1||
Z||000000||WhiteFieroBlur||12-13-2001||10:57 PM||||I am about to lower but I been hearing some horror stories about it. I have heard that your tires start to bend inward cause the cars lowered. I have seen it on my friends honda civic. Do all cars do this unless you upgrade the suspension?||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||12-13-2001||11:12 PM||||Well, you are *supposed* to get it aligned again after you lower it. I've had Eibach's and ST's and both could be aligned. 1-2" drop. <P>Drop spindles in the front don't have that problem (not having enough camber adjustment). Coilovers in the rear don't either.<P>If you go nuts and cut or heat the coils then you may wind up not being able to get the camber back in spec's even with Moog Problem Solver upper ball joints, but where that point is, I don't know. I figure it's just before you find out you cut too mich spring and you want to take them out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||qwikgta||12-13-2001||11:18 PM||||I cut off 1.5 coil from the front and 1.25 off the rears and I have not had a prob, but I have not had it alligned.  It is not that far off and it drives great.  I will need to get the MOOG fixers cause the camber is off in the front, and I want to be safe.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sig_Pic_copy.jpg">   <BR>"DRIVE IT LIKE YOU STOLE IT"<p>[This message has been edited by qwikgta (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tina||12-13-2001||11:32 PM||||No mechanical problem here either.<BR>One thing I found out recently though,<BR>those Full-Service Carwashes won't wash your car anymore.<BR>(Something about, it not being high enough  to clear the guide rails anymore.) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"><P>Tina  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||stingant0||12-13-2001||11:52 PM||||If you are going to do any autocrossing DON'T CUT!! It makes them very brittle and they can easly break. I have seen this happen to ricers that cut their springs.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||12-14-2001||12:52 AM||||You will need to get a full 4-wheel alignment. The MOOG ball joints should be used. I lowered mine 1.5" and used the MOOG's. I had a full alignment done and have had no problems for 2 years. Tell your friend to get an alignment after he gets new tires. Since he has been driving on them with the front out of alignment, the tires are now worn incorrectly.||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||12-14-2001||01:36 AM||||88s benefit greatly from cutting the springs, but DON'T do that on a non-88. (the 88s already are a 'coil over' shock assembley, just not an ajustable perch.)<P>No problems here, 88 car cut exactly one coil in front, lowering springs/struts in rear.<P>Works great, turns like a banshee on pcp, clears everything.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||fieroX||12-14-2001||03:32 AM||||I cut my springs 1.5 coils on both the front and rear.  My car is a 87.  I dont have problems with anything, I havent scraped on anything yet, the car handles better, it looks better and everything.  be sure and cut the springs with a cutoff wheel and not a torch, the torch can mess up the spring.  Good luck<P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||12-14-2001||07:40 AM||||Mine is stock ride height. I scrape on a lot of driveways and speed bumps, I can only imagine that lowering would make it worse.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||12-14-2001||08:02 AM||||Like everyone has said before, get the MOOG problem solver upper ball joints, that should help with the camber alignment.<P>Front drop spindles are the way to go if they fit in your budget.  None of the suspension geometry, ride travels are comprimised and you can keep the stock springs.  I have some pictures on my site.<P>Worried about ground clearance?  I wouldn't recommend anything more than 2" drop for street use.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerwinter3.gif">           <BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR><A HREF="http://www.envy.nu/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>Tigger's Fiero Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||12-14-2001||08:35 AM||||Mine is lowered with ST springs and full polyurethane.  Definitely go with the Moog balljoints and a quality alignment.<P>The only problems lowering mine has caused for me are continuous urges to take corners at high speeds.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Mine has been lowered for 7 years, and it's a daily driver.||||reg||1
Z||000011||CoolBlue87GT||12-14-2001||09:27 AM||||There's one thing that nobody has talked about yet ...<P>Lowered, would make it harder to get in & out of the car !!<P>With my stock height, I find it hard to get in & out of the car. <P>Maybe I'm taller than most ?<P>It's a lot lower than my daily driver (Ford Contour)<P>Good luck on your project.||||reg||6
Z||000012||Alex4mula||12-14-2001||12:41 PM||||Yeah, many problems. Each time my tall/fat friends get a ride they complain a lot of being on the floor and how hard is to get out. My wife complains a little too. Lowered Fieros has the right look. Specially pre 88s. Other than that is just ride quality preferences issues for your own.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||6
Z||000013||FLASHY||12-14-2001||01:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B>I cut my springs 1.5 coils on both the front and rear.  My car is a 87.  I dont have problems with anything, I havent scraped on anything yet, the car handles better, it looks better and everything.  be sure and cut the springs with a cutoff wheel and not a torch, the torch can mess up the spring.  Good luck<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Same situation for me. I agree. No problems for me either. Just get an alignment.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||MinnGreenGT||12-14-2001||01:28 PM||||Any suggestions from people as to the best place to pick up MOOG ball joints?||||reg||1
Z||000015||West Coast Fiero||12-14-2001||01:42 PM||||lowering is great and all, but what are you guys doing about bump stop relocation?<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000016||VISCERAL||12-14-2001||04:21 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.summitracing.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.summitracing.com</A>  call them up, they sell them at the best price.||||reg||1
Z||000017||JD86GT350||12-14-2001||07:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR><B>lowering is great and all, but what are you guys doing about bump stop relocation?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>hmm, that's somethin I've wondered about too||||reg||1
Z||000018||qwikgta||12-14-2001||10:32 PM||||Bumpstop??? OH, is that the rubber triangle thing that was getting in the way.  You don't have to relocate it, it comes off with only one 13MM nut on the underside of the A-arm.  Since it gets in the way just throw it in the trash.  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||fieroX||12-14-2001||10:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B><BR>Lowered, would make it harder to get in & out of the car !!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Im 6'3" and 205, I get in and out of my car about 30-40 times a day (I deliver pizzas for a living).  My car is lowered 1.5" and I dont have any problems.<BR>Now a friend of mine is 6'8" and 280 lbs, he has problems getting in and out. I can jump out of the car and have the door shut before he even gets his legs out.  And we rode to california last summer!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||West Coast Fiero||12-14-2001||10:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by qwikgta:<BR><B>Bumpstop??? OH, is that the rubber triangle thing that was getting in the way.  You don't have to relocate it, it comes off with only one 13MM nut on the underside of the A-arm.  Since it gets in the way just throw it in the trash.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>so what your saying is that it's ok when the wheel ( on up-travel ) blasts off the front fender when going over a speed bump, of course being worst case scenario <P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000021||fieroX||12-15-2001||02:19 AM||||how fast are you taking speed bumps to do damage like that?  <BR>I dont have any problems with bump stops, speed bumps, dips, roadkill or anything.  You guys worry too much.||||reg||1
Z||000022||rogergarrison||12-15-2001||06:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by qwikgta:<BR><B>Bumpstop??? OH, is that the rubber triangle thing that was getting in the way.  You don't have to relocate it, it comes off with only one 13MM nut on the underside of the A-arm.  Since it gets in the way just throw it in the trash.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ya, but if you hit a hard bump, its like taking a sledge hammer to the ball joint. Wont be long till it breaks, then your screwed.<P>||||reg||1
